  <title>7.10.2: Quadratic Forms and Their Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01904</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.10.2: Quadratic Forms and Their Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d381e68c-84f0-4af1-bfd5-318a703de9cd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>In an earlier lesson, we saw that an equation such as \(h(t)=10+78t-16t^2\) can model the height of an object thrown upward from a height of 10 feet with a vertical velocity of 78 feet per second.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d3ac68cd35ba82b9a19253c0b2542dc03eb45af0"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Is the expression \(-16t^2+78t+10\) written in standard form? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Yes. It is a sum of a squared term, a linear term, and a constant term. Written in the form \(ax^2+bx+c\), – 16 is \(a\), 78 is \(b\), and 10 is \(c\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Paola said that the equation \(g(t)=-2(8t+1)(t-5)\) also defines the same function, written in factored form. Is Paola correct? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Yes. Distribute the – 2 to \((8t+1)\) and you have \((-16t-2)\). Multiply this binomial by \((t-5)\). The result of \((-16t-2)(t-5)\) is \(-16t^2+78t+10\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Here is a graph representing both \(g(t)=-2(8t+1)(t-5)\) and \(h(t)=10+78t-16t^2\).
    
    <figure><media alt="Graph of non linear function."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d1dd5350da338c1dc1eb6b3988c725e481a1d9b7"/></media></figure></item>
</list>

<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="27b968da-8a11-4fd6-9367-a98f81e26fb8" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q3a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="930228bf-fe65-44d8-aee1-7dc87ea500a2" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="10">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept?</para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,10)\).</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q3b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="adf70d37-f2a9-40c1-b144-00dcb99942b0" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="5">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-value of the \(x\)-intercept?</para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(x\)-intercept is \((5,0)\).</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
